mcu, pcm: group the per-instruction and per-tick fields ahead of the bulk arrays (ARM)

An in-order AArch64 core is the target, and its addressing modes are the
point of this patch: a scaled 12-bit immediate reaches 16380 bytes for a
32-bit load and 4095 for a byte load. Upstream's mcu_t places 664 KiB of ROM
and RAM arrays between the register file and the fields the interpreter
touches on every instruction -- dev_register, the peripheral pointers, the
romset flags, rom2_mask, the operand-decode scratch -- so every one of those
loads needs its address materialised separately. pcm_t does the same to
enable_oversampling, which sat at offset 15,763,532 past 15 MB of wave ROM,
alone in a cache line, read once per sample.

Field order is layout only: neither struct is serialised, memcpy'd or
aggregate-initialised anywhere in the tree. The arrays move to the end and
everything hot lands inside the immediate's reach -- for mcu_t within the
first 344 bytes, the decoder's instruction-cache handle included.

On x86-64 this changes nothing measurable (32-bit displacements). On a
Cortex-A53 it is the largest single architecture-specific contribution in
the series: counting the instructions actually executed on the mk2 path
through MCU_Step, 37 upstream against 32 here, and measured as worst-second
realtime on a Raspberry Pi 3B, removing the mcu_t half costs 2.7% and the
pcm_t half 0.46%.

--- a/src/backend/mcu.h
+++ b/src/backend/mcu.h
@@ -232,7 +232,18 @@
 
 void MCU_DefaultSampleCallback(void* userdata, const AudioFrame<int32_t>& frame);
 
+// Field order here is deliberate: everything the interpreter touches on every
+// instruction is grouped ahead of the six memory arrays, which together are
+// 664 KiB. Upstream has the arrays in the middle, which pushes dev_register,
+// the peripheral pointers, the romset flags and the operand-decode scratch out
+// past a 656 KiB hole from the register file. That costs nothing on x86-64,
+// whose addressing modes take a 32-bit displacement, but on AArch64 a byte load
+// with a scaled 12-bit immediate reaches only 4095 bytes from its base, so
+// every one of those fields needs its address materialised separately. The
+// reorder is layout-only: mcu_t is never serialised, memcpy'd or aggregate
+// initialised anywhere in the tree.
 struct mcu_t {
+    // --- CPU state, touched by every instruction ---
     uint16_t r[8]{};
     uint16_t pc = 0;
     uint16_t sr = 0;
@@ -244,33 +255,15 @@
     BoundedOrderedBitSet<16> trapa_pending;
     uint64_t cycles = 0;
 
